  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "And the framers of our Constitution predicted some of this as they were writing the Federalist Papers, though I think that their concern was that there would be divisions by party lines if an impeachment came about, but not necessarily that they would just automatically exist. In reference to trials of elected leaders, Alexander Hamilton wrote in the Federalist Papers number 65, The prosecution of them, these elected officials, presumably a president, for this reason will seldom fail to agitate the passions of the whole community and to divide it into parties more or less friendly or inimical to the accused. In many cases, it will connect itself with the pre-existing factions and will enlist all their animosities, partialities, influence, and interest on one side or the other And in such cases, there will always be the greatest danger that the decision will be regulated more by the comparative strength of parties than by the real demonstrations of innocence or guilt. Are we in an era where adherence to a political party is going to permanently outweigh our duties to preserve the tenets of democracy that define our nation itself?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
