        "text": "Alexander Hamilton wrote in Federalist Paper No. 65 that prosecuting elected officials like a president would often stir public passion and divide the community into parties.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "When impeachment connects with pre-existing factions, the outcome may depend more on party strength than on evidence of innocence or guilt.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The framers of the Constitution anticipated that impeachment could lead to division along party lines.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "In many cases, impeachment proceedings would align with existing factions and mobilize their loyalties, biases, influences, and interests.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "We are currently living in an era where loyalty to political parties might permanently override civic duties to uphold democratic principles.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The integrity of democratic institutions depends on decisions being based on evidence rather than partisan allegiance.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Impeachment trials should ideally be impartial and guided by objective standards of justice.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Party affiliations have become deeply entrenched in American politics and shape people’s judgments even in high-stakes legal matters.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
