  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "He brags about it, right? I mean, he says, you know, I'm partial to the president and that's how I'm going to vote. That's a total defeat of the constitutional design. The constitutional design is it's a real trial. That's why the Chief Justice of the United States is there, instead of the Vice President. I mean, they didn't want the Vice President presiding over at the trial of the President, which obviously would have been awkward. But the Chief Justice would be there, and it's called a trial, and then it ends up in a conviction. You know, the president can be blocked from holding future civil office, so there's a punishment. Now, it's not criminal punishment, and that's an important thing for people to understand. This is not about putting the president in jail for a single day. It's not that kind of a trial, but it is a trial for the integrity of the office of the president and for protecting the country against a president who set himself against the rule of law and basically converted himself into a king. So, I think, yeah, the founders predicted a lot of this, and I mean, the founders were very interesting on the question of partisanship or what they called faction. They knew this was deep in human nature, and they talked a lot about human nature, and anybody You know, who's been, you know, part of any organization will see that factions form. Anybody who's been a member of a faculty will know what faculty meetings are like. You can't get human beings together without people dividing into different groups to fight about different things. And then we go into a different issue and then different groups form around that issue and fight about that. And so, okay, that's in human nature. They understood that. But what the framers were saying was that Let's try to come up with institutions that define roles, that define oaths of office, that obligate us to try to think about a higher role. I mean, take Thomas Jefferson. So Jefferson was a partisan brawler, as much in the mud as any of them were. I mean, he was a great political conniver for elections, and that 1800 election was unbelievable in terms of the stuff that took place. But in the final analysis, he knew that once we're in office, we have to try to rise above it. So in his inaugural address, he says, we are all Republicans. We are all federalists. I mean, that's a beautiful sentiment that you can follow through American history. I mean, that's like Lincoln saying, You know, we are not enemies. We are friends. We must not be enemies. Or even Obama saying, we're not the red states of America or the blue states of America. We're the United States of America. And so I liken it in my own small way to what happens when you get elected to Congress. Like you run with a party. You get in. But here in this office, we're in my district office right now. If somebody calls us with a problem with their VA benefits or Medicare or",
