  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 56,
    "turn_text": "We just go to work for them. And legislatively, I think we have to think like that, too. I'm not just out there voting to make life safer through gun safety policy for Democrats. I'm trying to make life safer for Republicans, for Democrats, for Greens, libertarians, independents, for everybody, whether or not they voted for me. And it's the same thing with the quality of life environmentally and foreign policy and so on. So we've got to have like two hats on. We've got to be strong, passionate, ferocious partisans in campaigns because that's how we organize the electorate. It's how we motivate people. It's how we articulate differences. It's how we develop programs that the public can vote on. But once we're in, I think we have to try to act as much as we can in the common good and for the public interest. And the impeachment thing really caught the reason it's the only other place where members of Congress were asked to take another oath. So we take an oath of office when we get in, but now you have to take another oath in a Senate impeachment trial. And there they say that, you know, I swear in all matters pertaining to the impeachment of Donald John Trump before the Senate to do impartial justice under the Constitution and the laws of the United States. So you're sworn in as a Senator slash juror at that point. And you have to have an open mind. And I keep saying this to the press. What McConnell and Lindsey Graham are talking about is basically jury nullification. And we've seen that throughout American history, when all white jury gets together and just says, we don't care about the facts. We don't care about the evidence. We don't care about the law. We don't care about what happened. If it's a black defendant, we're going to convict them. Okay, that is an absolute violation of the oath of office in addition to being discrimination. And that's what they're talking about doing. And oftentimes these reporters say to me, well, you know, they're Democrats, Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth Warren, they've said they think the president should be impeached. And to which I say, well, look, I think what they're saying, I hope what they're saying is they've seen overwhelming evidence based on what the House of Representatives have done. But they, once they take that oath of office, they must be 100% committed to being fair and impartial jurors.",
