    "turn_text": "much. I think all she's looking for, all the Democratic majority caucus is looking for in the House and I assume the Senate is the minimal requirements of a fair trial process, that there will be real evidence, that there will be the opportunity to call witnesses and so on. I think one way through this impasse would be for everybody to say, let's leave it to Chief Justice John Roberts in the first instance. He's the constitutionally I think that he would be fair-minded about the question of the relevance of various witnesses called, about the admission of evidence, about the subpoenaing of documents and witnesses. And in the final analysis, any of his rulings could be appealed to the full Senate, where we hope that people would render a fair dispassionate judgment. But I think for these purposes, we've got a trust in Chief Justice John Roberts, as much as I disagree with the number of things he's done as Chief Justice.",
