    "turn_idx": 98,
    "turn_text": "interest here is of a constitutional nature. The framers in their wisdom gave Congress the power to declare war. Why? Because the princes and the kings would plunge their nations into wars of vanity or political advantage just to distract the population from domestic political problems and to mobilize the people behind the king or the prince, the founders hated that dynamic and they thought it was wasteful and perilous for people and so they didn't think necessarily that war would end forever but they thought it should be the people's representatives who engage in the act of cost-benefit calculation about what do we gain from this and what do we lose by it and what are the risks of doing it going forward because who are the people who are being flown over there, who are the thousands of sons and daughters of America's families who are now put on the front lines of this conflict because the president decided from Mar-a-Lago that now was just the perfect time to assassinate an Iranian military general, you know.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
