  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 113,
    "turn_text": "I'm sure Trump will explain how he really got more electoral college votes than George Washington. But, you know, Washington made that same kind of point, though, where, you know, he used to say that the word party comes from the French word Pakti apart, and we have to remember that our party is just a part of the whole, and we get in, and we have to try to stand for the whole, and not just the part of it that got us elected, that helped. They helped to promote us. I think it's the wrong message if what we end up coming out of this with is just a fear or a hate for political parties. Political parties play their function, so I give them one or two cheers, not three cheers because they're not the be-all and the end-all. But what we do have in the Republican Party right now is a really retrograde philosophy that places victory above everything else. It's a rule of ruin philosophy. And they've helped to unleash and revive some of the darkest impulses from the 20th century. And, you know, what we saw in Charlottesville was a glimpse of what the future could be like in America, if we don't try to stand real strong against this ultra right-wing nationalism that is spreading around the world and that has come to America too.",
