  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 115,
    "turn_text": "And I see that both for historical reasons and contemporary reasons. I mean, Tocqueville said in Democracy in America that democracy is always either expanding and deepening or it's contracting. You got to move in one direction or the other. And we've been seeing democracy constricted and contracting. And, you know, we're seeing, you know, the president making deals with foreign powers and the voter suppression and the use of gerrymandering and all of these things to strangle democracy. But I've seen during our time the growth of these extraordinary pop-up civil society movements, you know, do the most good. Black Lives",
