  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 118,
    "turn_text": "A lot of what comes out of Washington and in Congress is a division of looking at the world as this is a Republican view of the world. This is a Democratic view of the world. Even some of what we've talked here today has been about the dangerous Republican view and approach of XYZ. Is it possible for us to let those barriers of division down and instead of painting people of one party or the other as having an entirely one approach to the world to talk about the forces of darkness and light. There's a contrast there, right? Part of the problem with Trump's ascension is He brings forward very comfortably a deep darkness about how he views the world and is ready to stroke that darkness in other people. And in some ways that has appealed to a base of people who are supporting the president because it helps justify their worldview that had been encouraged to evolve. It's also, unfortunately, appealed to the darkness within people on the Democratic side in how they're responding, in bringing out their worst approach with fear and anxiety and retribution rather than trying to have a place of understanding and compassion and love. And so it's not when these divisions are happening, I'd love for members of Congress to not just look at Republicans and say the Republicans this that or the other. There are people within the Republican Party whose darkest impulses are what is winning out right now. But we have darkness among members of Congress who are Democrats as well. We have Republicans that shine a giant heart of light and compassion in what they do. Can we get away from a politics that says the Republicans are this, the Democrats are that, and instead start having conversations about the emotional differences between how we enact in a civic way with an eye towards fear, jealousy, anger, or an eye towards love, compassion, connection?",
