  {
    "episode_id": 92347,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92347.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 121,
    "turn_text": "I don't mean in any sense to reify or deify the Democratic Party. I do think that right now we are on the side of a progressive direction in terms of a whole host of issues, including the overarching question of our times, which is climate change. as opposed to climate denialism, which unfortunately has found a home in the Republican Party, and has been adopted by their major leaders. So, I mean, I'm a hard-headed enough politician not to run away from the fact that different parties come to represent different things at different times. But your point is also absolutely correct, which is we should never confuse the partisan positions that are adopted by caucuses and the legislative positions that are adopted with the personalities or the character of individual people who are legislators and especially not people who are voters and people who are, you know, just out there. Because, you know, hey, we've got our friend Jennifer Lord, you know, who's Rebecca Lord's sister, you remember? Yes, of course. Jennifer is spending a couple weeks at her house. She's a conservative, fundamentalist, Christian, Republican from Texas. She's one of my favorite people in the world, and we hang out. We joke about politics, and we talk about politics, and I learn stuff from her. She learn stuff from me. So, you know, in some of my favorite people I've met in politics, like Lamar Smith from Texas, he was a conservative, you know, Republican. I think I moved him a little bit on the question of climate change, and he actually retired last year, but he shut down his campaign account, and he sent me $1,000, which I thought was a very",
